1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:00,960
[Music]

2
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:07,909
This is software engineering radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at se-radio.net.

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:00:12,070
SE radio is brought to you by the ITE computer society.

4
00:00:12,070 --> 00:00:15,790
and by its software magazine online at computer.org/software.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,109
[Music]

6
00:00:19,119 --> 00:00:21,109
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

7
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:23,590
I'm your host Jeff Doolittle.

8
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:29,029
This episode will explore the role of human programmers in the AI era.

9
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:33,670
Topics around machine learning and AI have come up frequently in previous episodes.

10
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:43,030
Some examples are episode 633 with Itamar Freriedman on automated testing with generative AI.

11
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:49,110
Episode 63 hosted Rish Singh on using Genai for test code generation.

12
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:57,270
And in episode 626, EPZ Oskaya joined software engineering radio again to discuss Gen AI for software architecture.

13
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:04,149
Today we're hosting Arty Chevchenko who is a former Google software engineer, exceed and founder of a startup.

14
00:01:04,149 --> 00:01:09,030
Currently he's a software engineer at Canva and a lecturer at ITMO University.

15
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:13,270
which is famous for winning more programming world cups than any other university in the world.

16
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:17,510
He is also the author of the recently published book Code Health Guardian.

17
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:23,031
the old new role of human programmers in the AI era. Arty, welcome to the show.

18
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,511
Thanks for having me. Glad you're here.

19
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:27,670
The subtitle of your book, as I said,

20
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:33,109
is the old new role of human programmers in the AI era.

21
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:38,230
In your view, what does it take for programmers to be successful in the era of AI?

22
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:43,670
I think there are three things you need to be great at.

23
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:46,951
One is collaborating with AI. Another is delegating to AI.

24
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,590
These two are very much on the hype right now.

25
00:01:51,590 --> 00:01:54,310
Everybody talks about Copilot and Cursor, I guess.

26
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:57,109
But there is also one more thing there.

27
00:01:57,109 --> 00:02:00,550
You really want to be better than AI at something.

28
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:05,350
And if we were talking about software engineers,

29
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:09,749
I would say your best bet is to become an expert in managing code complexity.

30
00:02:09,749 --> 00:02:12,710
Just because complex reasoning is hard for AI.

31
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:18,150
and in software engineering, that's probably the hardest problem we have.

32
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:20,949
How do we keep our code bases reasonably simple?

33
00:02:20,949 --> 00:02:29,030
So yeah, honestly I wouldn't over focus on learning these AI tools like cursor and copilot today.

34
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:31,670
and really spend some time to make sure.

35
00:02:31,670 --> 00:02:34,309
that you're ready for this new role of human software engineers.

36
00:02:34,319 --> 00:02:40,150
which is code health guardian that's how I call it and that's the title of the book.

37
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:42,710
So you mentioned the idea of complexity.

38
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:44,790
In case it's unclear to our listeners, help us connect.

39
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:50,949
How does complexity relate to this idea that you describe about programmers as code health guardians?

40
00:02:50,959 --> 00:02:52,949
Yeah, definitely.

41
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:58,949
When I say code health, I basically mean code that is simple enough,

42
00:02:58,949 --> 00:03:01,110
code that is reasonably simple or maintainable.

43
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:08,869
So if that's the hardest problem in software engineering to keep our code bases simple enough.

44
00:03:08,869 --> 00:03:15,830
then it's likely that we will continue to be better than AI in this for at least some.

45
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:19,110
time maybe years maybe even decades.

46
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:20,949
so yeah that's basically the link there.

47
00:03:20,959 --> 00:03:24,869
why does AI struggle to keep things simple in your experience?

48
00:03:24,879 --> 00:03:28,070
complex reasoning is hard for AI.

49
00:03:28,070 --> 00:03:33,430
so it looks simple on the surface often I I think that's what AI is very good at.

50
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:38,550
It gives you an impression that it understands things, right?

51
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:42,789
And it gives you an impression that okay, that's super simple.

52
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:46,470
It's like 1 2 3 here you go.

53
00:03:46,470 --> 00:03:52,710
But sometimes the real world has very significant challenges that need to be deeply understood.

54
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:58,470
and that's what humans still do better than AI at this point.

55
00:03:58,470 --> 00:04:04,231
So would you say sometimes the AI comes up with simplistic solutions rather than simple ones? Okay. Okay.

56
00:04:04,231 --> 00:04:13,350
So your contention is that humans still need to maintain the sense of complexity management.

57
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:15,429
and the way we do it is by guarding the health of our code.

58
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:18,150
Yeah. Exactly. And you mentioned maintainability.

59
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,469
Are there other aspects to code health?

60
00:04:20,469 --> 00:04:23,670
You mentioned simplicity and maintainability.

61
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:26,710
How else would you characterize code that is healthy?

62
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:37,510
That's basically the definition. If we're talking about definitions, I just use the term health as an alternative to saying.

63
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:40,230
that is reasonably simple or pragmatically simple.

64
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:44,790
But I like to think about code health as a productivity question.

65
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:47,749
I think it is ultimately a productivity question.

66
00:04:47,749 --> 00:04:53,830
So if you think about human productivity or programmers productivity,

67
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:56,950
the first thing that comes to mind is team productivity, right?

68
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:01,350
There are a bunch of factors that make team either productive or not productive.

69
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:10,550
like psychological safety, reliability, clear goals, clear responsibilities, impact, all these kind of things.

70
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:16,629
But if we zoom in a little bit into an individual in a team,

71
00:05:16,629 --> 00:05:19,749
still in a reasonably productive team.

72
00:05:19,749 --> 00:05:24,150
you will have individuals which perform really great and individuals that perform not that great.

73
00:05:24,150 --> 00:05:31,830
And historically, if you're an engineering manager dealing with this issue, the question you're asking yourself is.

74
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:38,550
well, is it a skill problem or is it a will problem, right?

75
00:05:38,550 --> 00:05:41,590
But I find that often the answer to this question is no.

76
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:46,710
It may be all good with skills and it may be all good with the will.

77
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:51,830
So for me, I don't think I ever had any major issues with the will problems.

78
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:55,270
or skill problems.

79
00:05:55,270 --> 00:06:02,310
For me, I definitely had some issues with finding the right balance between long-term and short-term productivity.

80
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,350
And that's what I call term productivity.

81
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:09,749
It's pretty hard to talk without any visuals.

82
00:06:09,749 --> 00:06:15,029
But if you can imagine having a spectrum where on the left side it is short-term productivity.

83
00:06:15,039 --> 00:06:20,150
and on the right side it is long-term productivity.

84
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:23,430
If you're too focused on the long-term productivity, it's kind of obvious why you're not productive.

85
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:26,629
because you're often planning to be productive in the future, right?

86
00:06:26,639 --> 00:06:28,230
Someday I'll be productive, right?

87
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:31,110
Yeah.

88
00:06:31,110 --> 00:06:36,150
And uh if you're focused too much on the short term, you become too tactical.

89
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:41,590
you create too much complexity along the way and that's what AI agents could do for us too.

90
00:06:41,590 --> 00:06:43,270
And you really want to have the balance there.

91
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:49,670
You want to care about users needs right now to deliver now.

92
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:51,990
but also care about user needs like a year from now.

93
00:06:51,990 --> 00:06:54,309
so that you don't create too much complexity.

94
00:06:54,309 --> 00:06:56,790
so that you cannot execute in a year from now.

95
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,550
Right.

96
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,710
So a few interesting insights from that one.

97
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:02,870
You didn't seem to say that long-term or short-term is better.

98
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:07,350
There's a balance between long and short-term productivity. Did I understand that correctly?

99
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,189
Yeah, that's correct.

100
00:07:09,189 --> 00:07:14,390
And definitely there are times when you need to focus on the short term for several weeks.

101
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:16,550
or maybe even months and just deliver.

102
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:21,350
There are times when you need to focus more on long-term.

103
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:26,550
but in general, you need to find the healthy balance between these two extremes. and be somewhere in the middle.

104
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:28,790
Okay.

105
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:31,350
And then the other aspect I think you're implying as well is.

106
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:36,870
that it's humans job to ensure long-term productivity.

107
00:07:36,870 --> 00:07:41,510
That AI might make us short-term productive, but that at least as it currently stands.

108
00:07:41,510 --> 00:07:45,511
it's not going to solve the problem for us of long-term productivity. Is that correct?

109
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:48,309
Yeah, exactly. Okay, great.

110
00:07:48,309 --> 00:07:49,990
Well, that might lead really well into my next question,

111
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:55,029
which is what motivated you to write Code Health Guardian?

112
00:07:55,029 --> 00:08:00,869
Well, I guess I was always passionate about knowledge sharing.

113
00:08:00,869 --> 00:08:07,990
So, even starting with my uni years, I remember we were doing classes for high school students.

114
00:08:07,990 --> 00:08:16,550
and even primary school students a little bit and right now I'm teaching in university doing university classes.

115
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:21,909
So, yeah, this passion for knowledge sharing was pretty much always there.

116
00:08:21,909 --> 00:08:27,029
But in terms of this particular book, I guess there are three key events there.

117
00:08:27,029 --> 00:08:30,950
apart from doing university classes which definitely prepared me for writing this book.

118
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:37,269
because they inspired the content of those classes inspired the content of the book very very much.

119
00:08:37,269 --> 00:08:41,029
But there are two other key events I would mention.

120
00:08:41,039 --> 00:08:48,150
The first one is doing good code reviewer internal classes at Google.

121
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:53,670
So the story there is that when I was at Google, I was pretty much every day.

122
00:08:53,670 --> 00:08:57,750
I was just spending a little bit of time reading random documentation.

123
00:08:57,750 --> 00:09:00,389
and at some point I started realizing.

124
00:09:00,389 --> 00:09:06,550
that there is so much information about how to do code reviews on our internet.

125
00:09:06,550 --> 00:09:08,550
and I just wanted to consolidate all of that.

126
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:10,310
So that's what I did.

127
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:15,430
I just consolidated all of that, created a single slide deck for that.

128
00:09:15,430 --> 00:09:17,829
And at some point that slide deck became pretty popular.

129
00:09:17,829 --> 00:09:25,430
and I even started getting requests from other Googlers asking whether there are any tech talks about this.

130
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:28,310
if there are any classes about this.

131
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:35,990
So I started doing the good code reviewer classes internally at Google and that was pretty successful.

132
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:38,230
I think in just one year we had over a thousand Googlers,

133
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:43,190
software engineers who went through those classes.

134
00:09:43,190 --> 00:09:45,990
We got like astonishing 4.5 rating based on over 100 reviews.

135
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:50,870
and that's really golden standard at Google for tech talks.

136
00:09:50,870 --> 00:09:56,230
Like you know when we're doing on boarding that's roughly the rating you get for those on boarding.

137
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:58,150
classes.

138
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:01,350
and like if you can if you can reach 4.5 that's kind of. that means you've done a good job.

139
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:04,070
Yeah.

140
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:07,110
So yeah, that was the first key event doing those classes.

141
00:10:07,110 --> 00:10:12,470
It gave me quite a lot of confidence I guess to start university courses later.

142
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:21,190
The second key event was John Asterhood book presentation in Google's Sunnyville office.

143
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:24,310
So I just randomly ended up being there.

144
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:26,949
I had no idea what to expect.

145
00:10:26,949 --> 00:10:29,829
I guess it was just advertised by some internal channels.

146
00:10:29,829 --> 00:10:32,230
and I ended up being in that room just knowing.

147
00:10:32,230 --> 00:10:36,949
that some guy is going to present on his software engineering book.

148
00:10:36,949 --> 00:10:39,670
And when he was talking about software engineering.

149
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:46,230
and complexity and complexity causes and complexity symptoms as he called them.

150
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:53,509
that really resonated with me and was like, "Oh yeah, that really makes sense."

151
00:10:53,509 --> 00:10:56,630
And yeah then when I was starting the university classes.

152
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:04,710
I think to a large extent maybe up to 50% the initial content of those university courses was.

153
00:11:04,710 --> 00:11:07,190
actually based on John's book philosophy of software design.

154
00:11:07,190 --> 00:11:13,030
Also of course my own experience at Google and a bunch of other books like pragmatic programmer.

155
00:11:13,030 --> 00:11:14,550
but John's book was very very key.

156
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:16,310
That's great.

157
00:11:16,310 --> 00:11:21,269
And I'll put a link in the show notes to software engineering radio episode 520.

158
00:11:21,269 --> 00:11:25,590
where I interviewed John Osterhout about his book a philosophy of software design.

159
00:11:25,590 --> 00:11:28,230
So that'll be good for listeners to be able to dig into.

160
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:32,069
So a lot of our guests on the show have written books.

161
00:11:32,069 --> 00:11:35,590
and my understanding having not published a book myself is that it can be challenging.

162
00:11:35,590 --> 00:11:37,990
So what helped you to stick with it along the way?

163
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,430
Yeah, it was a long long process.

164
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:45,350
I started it in 2022.

165
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:49,910
At that point, software engineering at Google book was already published.

166
00:11:49,910 --> 00:11:53,030
and a bunch of things that I've learned internally at Google.

167
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:57,590
I was surprised that they were not covered.

168
00:11:57,590 --> 00:12:01,110
because I was expecting it to be a lot about actually coding.

169
00:12:01,110 --> 00:12:03,430
and software design and all this stuff.

170
00:12:03,430 --> 00:12:05,430
how we do this internally at Google.

171
00:12:05,430 --> 00:12:09,350
But if you take a look at the Flamingo book, there is not much about it there.

172
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:11,829
It's mostly about building engineering organization.

173
00:12:11,839 --> 00:12:17,829
Okay. So that gave me a feeling that there is a an opportunity there.

174
00:12:17,829 --> 00:12:22,069
Already at that point I been iterating on the university classes for quite a long time.

175
00:12:22,069 --> 00:12:31,829
So I've had quite a lot of insights into how to communicate complex topics in a simple way.

176
00:12:31,829 --> 00:12:35,110
but also some unique insights about software design as well.

177
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:40,390
And in 2022 when I suddenly got quite a lot of time I decided to start writing the. book.

178
00:12:40,390 --> 00:12:47,910
So I did a couple of chapters and one of the chapters was on design docs.

179
00:12:47,910 --> 00:12:53,590
So I reached out to Max Canada Alexander the author of code simplicity with.

180
00:12:53,590 --> 00:12:56,150
that draft asking for his feedback.

181
00:12:56,150 --> 00:13:00,790
And I guess that's one of the turning points when you want to get support from somebody.

182
00:13:00,790 --> 00:13:05,430
because you're kind of like you're just starting and you're not completely sure how will it go.

183
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:11,990
And what Max did he actually referred me to Titus Winters.

184
00:13:11,990 --> 00:13:14,230
who was saying look for this you probably need to talk to Titus.

185
00:13:14,230 --> 00:13:18,470
He was curating software engineering at Google and he can give you a feedback for this.

186
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:19,990
So that's what I did.

187
00:13:19,990 --> 00:13:22,389
I asked him for a warm intro.

188
00:13:22,389 --> 00:13:28,230
He did me a warm winter and I sent it to Titus and Titus was so supportive.

189
00:13:28,230 --> 00:13:33,829
He looked through the chapter draft and said that well look it looks great.

190
00:13:33,829 --> 00:13:40,389
For different reasons, we decided to keep the design docs section in software engineer and Google pretty short.

191
00:13:40,389 --> 00:13:45,111
But yeah, it's definitely worth making it public and talking about this to. which I responded to him saying like,

192
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:51,590
"Hey, do you want to write this together, write this book together, maybe overly ambitious.

193
00:13:51,590 --> 00:13:56,949
but at that point I already went through the YC combinator startup school and was running a startup.

194
00:13:56,949 --> 00:14:00,310
So like I was a little bit pushy, but Titus said like that would be great.

195
00:14:00,310 --> 00:14:02,790
That's a great project, but I don't really have time for this.

196
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:08,550
Anyway, Titus support was very important in the beginning.

197
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:14,150
I guess that what made me brave enough to continue working on it.

198
00:14:14,150 --> 00:14:18,150
I guess the second turning point was when after a year of writing,

199
00:14:18,150 --> 00:14:22,629
I realized that the scope is a little bit too big, you know.

200
00:14:22,629 --> 00:14:25,750
because originally the name of the book was productive software engineering.

201
00:14:25,750 --> 00:14:28,629
That's what I wanted to. That's the name of the university.

202
00:14:28,629 --> 00:14:31,189
Broad it is.

203
00:14:31,189 --> 00:14:35,111
Yeah, but that's the university course and I was thinking maybe I I would just publish my university. course.

204
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:36,629
and it has two parts.

205
00:14:36,629 --> 00:14:41,509
It has this technical part which is about code complexity and there is like nontechnical part.

206
00:14:41,519 --> 00:14:47,509
but after a year I realized yeah that's too much. So it would be talmud if if you can say it in English.

207
00:14:47,519 --> 00:14:51,590
right that's the word yeah a very very long book.

208
00:14:51,590 --> 00:14:57,750
so I've reduced the scope and decided to focus solely on code health.

209
00:14:57,750 --> 00:15:01,030
and decided let it be a story of a healthy codebase.

210
00:15:01,030 --> 00:15:06,310
so that was the second part that really helped actually complete this work.

211
00:15:06,310 --> 00:15:11,910
and the third one the third turning point not a turning point.

212
00:15:11,910 --> 00:15:14,150
but I received a lot of support from tech reviewers.

213
00:15:14,150 --> 00:15:15,990
So that's something I didn't expect.

214
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:18,230
but there were so many like dozens of people.

215
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:24,870
who left a bunch of comments on the manuscript and helped to make this book much better.

216
00:15:24,870 --> 00:15:27,269
So yeah, I guess these three events or turning points.

217
00:15:27,279 --> 00:15:29,430
Great.

218
00:15:29,430 --> 00:15:33,829
Can you share a real world example of a project or a team that neglected code health.

219
00:15:33,829 --> 00:15:36,069
and it led to significant productivity issues?

220
00:15:36,079 --> 00:15:38,550
Yeah, sure.

221
00:15:38,550 --> 00:15:41,750
I think actually these stories is what made me truly pragmatic.

222
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:46,629
and truly care about code health to be like strategic about code complexity.

223
00:15:46,629 --> 00:15:51,189
not on one of the extremes on the term productivity spectrum.

224
00:15:51,189 --> 00:15:57,350
and that's story is from my startup from the company I was running.

225
00:15:57,350 --> 00:15:59,670
I think that was really really motivating not only.

226
00:15:59,670 --> 00:16:06,069
because I as a CEO cared about product so much and productivity.

227
00:16:06,069 --> 00:16:08,710
but also that because it was a self-funded startup.

228
00:16:08,710 --> 00:16:13,910
So, I was actually paying engineers to do the work.

229
00:16:13,910 --> 00:16:19,670
